00:03:05.960 --> 00:03:12.600
Imagine you allocated your entire TFSA last month into a single Canadian bank stock after

00:03:12.600 --> 00:03:14.200
seeing strong earnings growth.

00:03:14.640 --> 00:03:19.960
Your order filled at the prevailing price, yet the position later declined 6% while a

00:03:19.960 --> 00:03:25.780
balanced ETF holding the same name plus bonds and international equities fell only 2%.

00:03:26.200 --> 00:03:28.200
The mechanism at work is correlation.

00:03:28.960 --> 00:03:35.120
When one asset class moves sharply, an undiversified portfolio amplifies the swing because every

00:03:35.120 --> 00:03:37.040
dollar shares the same risk factor.

00:03:38.120 --> 00:03:43.060
Professionals reduce this exposure by mapping each holding to its expected volatility and

00:03:43.060 --> 00:03:48.180
covariance with other assets, then solving for the mix that sits on the efficient frontier.

00:03:48.940 --> 00:03:56.920
A concrete Canadian example is holding XEQT for full equity exposure, VGRO for a 60% equity

00:03:56.920 --> 00:04:03.100
and 40% fixed income blend, or XBAL for a more conservative 50-50 split.

00:04:03.100 --> 00:04:09.320
Each rebalance is automatically, so investors avoid the common error of letting winners run unchecked.

00:04:09.740 --> 00:04:17.620
The pro tip most retail accounts miss is checking the actual correlation matrix rather than assuming bonds always offset stocks.

00:04:18.220 --> 00:04:22.940
Post 2022 data shows that protection weakened when inflation surprised higher.

00:04:23.680 --> 00:04:30.080
The misconception to avoid is treating age-based rules like 100-year-age in stocks as precise.

00:04:30.080 --> 00:04:35.540
price. Instead, rebalance once a year or when any sleeve drifts more than 5 percentage

00:04:35.540 --> 00:04:40.640
points from target. This week's simulated weekly hold targets National Bank of Canada.

